<h2>Indian red onion for export</h2>
<p>India is one of the world's largest onion exporters, and Nashik Red is the variety most buyers mean when they ask for Indian onion. It's deep red, stores reasonably well when properly cured from the rabi crop, and feeds established trade routes into Bangladesh, Malaysia, Sri Lanka, Nepal and the Gulf.</p>

<p>We source primarily from Maharashtra — Nashik, Ahmednagar, Pune and Solapur — with Karnataka and Gujarat supplementing in specific windows. Onion is graded to your size specification, packed in mesh bags for airflow, and loaded into ventilated containers.</p>

<h2>Important: Indian onion export policy changes frequently</h2>
<p>This deserves its own section because it affects how you should read any onion price quote — from us or anyone else.</p>
<p>India actively manages onion exports to protect domestic supply and consumer prices. Over recent years that has included a Minimum Export Price (a legal floor price below which exports aren't permitted), an export duty, and in 2023–24 an outright export ban lasting roughly six months. These measures have been introduced, modified and withdrawn at short notice.</p>
<p><strong>What this means practically:</strong> we confirm the applicable export conditions — MEP, duty, and any licensing requirement — at the time your order is placed, and your contract reflects the terms in force then. We won't quote you a fixed onion price months ahead as though policy were settled, because it isn't. If you're planning a recurring onion programme, build that variability into your sourcing plan and talk to us about how to structure it.</p>

<h2>How should I specify onion?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Size grade.</strong> 40–60mm, 50–70mm or 60–80mm are the common export bands. Markets differ — pick your grade explicitly rather than accepting "export quality" as a spec.</li>
  <li><strong>Curing.</strong> Properly cured onion with dry outer skin and a firm neck is what survives transit. Under-cured stock rots. This is the single biggest quality variable on an onion shipment.</li>
  <li><strong>Packing.</strong> Mesh/net bags (5–25kg) allow airflow. Sealed packaging traps moisture and causes rot — we don't recommend it for onion regardless of what a buyer's retail programme might prefer.</li>
</ul>

